# Which opening traps catch club players?
teaches: ["openings/traps"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | calm, warm chess coach","b":"gemini:Puck | eager club player, relaxed and good-humoured; never exaggerated"}
prompt: "How do the Fried Liver, Légal's mate and the Blackburne–Shilling trap work? How do you avoid each one, and when can you use them?"
next: {"title":"What do the pawns tell you?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/chess-30-days/19-what-do-the-pawns-tell-you.cast"}

## Warm-up: a fork and a pin
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: rnb1kbnr/pp1pqppp/8/1Np1p3/4P3/8/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKBNR w KQkq - 0 4, moves: [Nc7+, Kd8, Nxa8]}
      - {fen: r1bqk2r/pppp1p1p/3b4/4n1p1/2B5/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQR1K1 w kq - 0 8, moves: [d4]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0

    card title Warm-up

A: Today: three opening traps that catch club players, plus two more for you to find. How each one works, how to dodge it, and when to set it.
    draw position

A: Every trap hides an old tactic, so warm up with two: a fork, then a pin.
    highlight piece_e8

B: Nothing's attacked. I'd just develop: bishop to c4.
    highlight piece_f1

A: Three Questions first. Black's queen has just left d8.
    highlight piece_e7

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["A knight move with check.","Which square touches the king and the rook in the corner?"] themes ["fork"] voice a teaches ["tactics/fork"]
        right: Knight to c7, check, hits the king and the rook. The king steps to d8, and the knight takes the rook on a8.
        wrong: Look for a knight check that hits something big as well.

B: And with bishop to c4, I'd have missed a whole rook.
    highlight piece_a8

A: Second one. Black's knight has jumped to e5.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

B: It's hitting my bishop on c4. I'll move the bishop.
    highlight piece_e5 piece_c4

A: Look down the e-file first. Behind that knight stands Black's own king.
    highlight piece_e1 piece_e5 piece_e8

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["The knight can't leave the e-file.","Attack it with a pawn."] themes ["pin"] voice a teaches ["tactics/pin"]
        right: Pawn to d4 attacks the knight, and it can't step off the e-file. A pawn wins a piece.
        wrong: The knight can't move. What cheap piece can attack it?

B: So the knight attacking me was the one in trouble.
    highlight piece_e5

A: A fork and a pin. Each of today's traps hides one of them in the first few moves.
    highlight piece_d4 piece_e5

## Three traps
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1, moves: [e4, e5, Nf3, Nc6, Bc4, Nd4, Nxe5, Qg5, Nxf7, Qxg2, Rf1, Qxe4+, Be2, Nf3#]}
      - {fen: rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1, moves: [e4, e5, Nf3, d6, Bc4, Bg4, Nc3, g6, Nxe5, Bxd1, Bxf7+, Ke7, Nd5#]}
      - {fen: rn1qkbnr/ppp2p1p/3p2p1/4N3/2B1P1b1/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQK2R b KQkq - 0 5, moves: [dxe5, Qxg4]}
      - {fen: rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1, moves: [e4, e5, Nf3, Nc6, Bc4, Nf6, Ng5, d5, exd5, Nxd5, Nxf7, Kxf7, Qf3+, Ke6, Nc3], flip: true, arrows: [{from: c6, to: a5}]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 5
sources:
    - id: bs
      title: Blackburne Shilling Gambit
      authors: Wikipedia contributors
      year: 2026
      finding: 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bc4 Nd4; 4.Nxe5? Qg5 5.Nxf7?? Qxg2 6.Rf1 Qxe4+ 7.Be2 Nf3#. Named from the legend that Blackburne won a shilling a game with it; Bill Wall notes no recorded Blackburne games with the line. Steinitz (1895) recommended 4.Nxd4 or 4.O-O and called 5.Bxf7+ White's best chance after 4.Nxe5 Qg5.
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blackburne_Shilling_Gambit
    - id: legal
      title: Légal Trap
      authors: Wikipedia contributors
      year: 2026
      finding: 'Légal (Legall de Kermeur) vs Saint Brie, Paris, about 1750 (the date is uncertain; perhaps 1787), played at rook odds: 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 d6 3.Bc4 Bg4 4.Nc3 g6 5.Nxe5 Bxd1 6.Bxf7+ Ke7 7.Nd5#; the knight sacrifice wins only if Black takes the queen.'
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/L%C3%A9gal_Trap
    - id: legall
      title: Legall de Kermeur
      authors: Wikipedia contributors
      year: 2026
      finding: François Antoine de Legall de Kermeur (1702–1792), the leading player at the Café de la Régence in Paris and Philidor's teacher.
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Legall_de_Kermeur
    - id: fried
      title: Fried liver attack
      authors: Wikipedia contributors
      year: 2026
      finding: 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bc4 Nf6 4.Ng5 d5 5.exd5 Nxd5?! 6.Nxf7 Kxf7 7.Qf3+ Ke6 8.Nc3; named after fegatelli, liver wrapped in netting; strong attack not proven decisive, hard to defend at short time controls; 5...Na5 generally considered best.
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fried_liver_attack

    card title "Three traps"

A: Now that the tactics are warm: the first trap comes in your own Italian.
    draw position

A: Black's knight jumps to d4 and leaves e5 unguarded.
    animate plies_shown 6 duration 1.3

B: A free pawn! And my knight hits f7 too.
    animate plies_shown 7 duration 1.3

A: That grab is fine. But queen to g5 hits two things:
    animate plies_shown 8 duration 1.3

A: your knight, and the pawn on g2.
    highlight piece_e5 piece_g2

A: Which move walks into the trap?
    highlight piece_g5

    quiz "Which move walks into the trap?" on_canvas true say_question false buttons_at.x 915 buttons_at.y 400 buttons_layout column voice a
        right: Knight takes f7: it forks the queen and the rook, but the queen escapes to g2 and hits your rook.
        wrong: After your move, what can the queen take, and what does she hit then?
        + Nxf7
        * Bxf7+
        * Ng4

B: I'd still take f7. It forks the queen and the rook!
    animate plies_shown 9 duration 1.3

A: Queen takes g2, out of the fork, and your rook runs to f1.
    blocking false

    animate plies_shown 10 duration 1.2

    pause 0.6

    animate plies_shown 11 duration 1

A: Queen takes e4, check. If the bishop blocks,
    blocking false

    animate plies_shown 12 duration 1.2

    pause 0.4

    animate plies_shown 13 duration 1

A: knight to f3 is mate. Your own pieces wall in the king.
    animate plies_shown 14 duration 1.3

A: The story goes that the English master Joseph Blackburne won shillings with this trap. No game of his with it has been found.
    highlight piece_e1

A: To skip it all, answer knight to d4 by taking it, or by castling: that was Steinitz's advice.
    animate plies_shown 6 duration 0.6

A: Second trap, whose finish you met on day 12: Légal's mate.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 8 duration 0.4

A: Here's how it starts: Black pins your knight to your queen.
    highlight piece_g4 piece_f3 piece_d1

B: So the knight can't move.
    highlight piece_f3

A: It can: a pin to the queen isn't a pin to the king. Knight takes e5 anyway.
    animate plies_shown 9 duration 1.3

B: And Black grabs the queen.
    animate plies_shown 10 duration 1.3

A: Bishop takes f7, check, king to e7, and knight to d5 is mate.
    blocking false

    animate plies_shown 11 duration 1

    animate plies_shown 12 duration 1

    animate plies_shown 13 duration 1

    pause 0.5

    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: A careful Black takes the knight instead. Then your queen takes the loose bishop, and you're still a pawn up: that's why it's safe to set.
    blocking false

    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.3

    pause 1.2

    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.3

B: And as Black: before I grab a queen, I look for the checks that follow.
    highlight piece_c4

A: Now you're Black, so the board turns. Our bishop to c5 avoids this one; knight to f6 invites it.
    animate position_shown 3 plies_shown 6 duration 0.4

A: Knight to g5 aims two pieces at f7.
    animate plies_shown 7 duration 1.3

B: I block with pawn to d5. White takes, and I take back with the knight.
    blocking false

    animate plies_shown 8 duration 1.1

    animate plies_shown 9 duration 1.1

    animate plies_shown 10 duration 1.1

A: Knight takes f7, forking your queen and rook.
    animate plies_shown 11 duration 1.3

A: The king takes, and queen to f3 is check, hitting your knight on d5.
    blocking false

    animate plies_shown 12 duration 1.1

    animate plies_shown 13 duration 1.3

    pause 0.3

    ask "Don't lose the knight." widget chess line true hints ["A king move that guards d5.","Count the guards on d5 after each king move."] themes ["defence","opening"] voice a
        right: King to e6: the only square that guards the knight. Then White's other knight comes to c3 to hit d5, and your king stands in the middle of the board.
        wrong: The knight is pinned and attacked twice. Which king move guards it?

A: That's the Fried Liver, named after an Italian dish of liver wrapped in a net, like this king. It isn't a forced win, but it's miserable to defend at speed.
    highlight piece_e6

    pause 0.6

    animate plies_shown 9 duration 0.6

A: The cure: after pawn takes d5, play knight to a5, not knight takes. And as White, knight to g5 sets it for you.
    draw arrow_0

    erase arrow_0

A: Three traps, one shape: a move that looks free, and the punishment one move deeper.
    highlight piece_d5

## Your turn: spring and dodge
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: Four for four. Your opponents should be nervous.
    good: Most of them. The rest will turn up in your games.
    poor: Traps hide well. Run the Three Questions and try again.
    none: The traps will wait for you.
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: r1b1kbnr/pppp1Npp/8/6q1/2BnP3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R b KQkq - 0 5, moves: [Qxg2], flip: true}
      - {fen: r2qkbnr/ppp2ppp/3p4/4n2b/2B1P3/2N4P/PPPP1PP1/R1BQK2R w KQkq - 0 7, moves: [Qxh5]}
      - {fen: rnbqkb1r/pppp1ppp/5n2/4N3/8/8/PPPPQPPP/RNB1KB1R w KQkq - 2 5, moves: [Nc6+, Qe7, Nxe7, Bxe7]}
      - {fen: rnbqk1nr/ppp2ppp/8/4P3/1BP5/4p3/PP3PPP/RN1QKBNR b KQkq - 0 6, moves: [exf2+, Ke2, fxg1=N+], flip: true}
      - {fen: r1bqkb1r/ppp2ppp/2n2n2/3Pp1N1/2B5/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R b KQkq - 0 5, moves: [], flip: true}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0
sources:
    - id: bs
      title: Blackburne Shilling Gambit
      authors: Wikipedia contributors
      year: 2026
      finding: After 3...Nd4 4.Nxe5 Qg5 5.Nxf7? Qxg2 6.Rf1 Qxe4+ 7.Be2 Nf3# — a smothered mate.
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blackburne_Shilling_Gambit
    - id: legal
      title: Légal Trap
      authors: Wikipedia contributors
      year: 2026
      finding: In the line 4...Bg4 5.h3 Bh5 6.Nxe5, Black's better options are 6...Nxe5 or 6...dxe5 rather than taking the queen.
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/L%C3%A9gal_Trap
    - id: petrov
      title: Petrov's Defence
      authors: Wikipedia contributors
      year: 2026
      finding: 3.Nxe5 Nxe4 4.Qe2 Nf6?? 5.Nc6+ wins the queen.
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Petrov%27s_Defence
    - id: lasker
      title: Lasker Trap
      authors: Wikipedia contributors
      year: 2026
      finding: 'Albin Countergambit: 1.d4 d5 2.c4 e5 3.dxe5 d4 4.e3 Bb4+ 5.Bd2 dxe3 6.Bxb4? exf2+ 7.Ke2 fxg1=N+; named after Emanuel Lasker, first noted by Serafino Dubois.'
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lasker_Trap
    - id: fried
      title: Fried liver attack
      authors: Wikipedia contributors
      year: 2026
      finding: After 4.Ng5 d5 5.exd5, 5...Na5 is generally considered best; 5...Nxd5 allows 6.Nxf7.
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fried_liver_attack

    card title "Your turn"

A: Now that you've seen the three traps, spring them yourself, plus two more. The board turns: you're Black, in the Blackburne–Shilling.
    draw position

B: White grabbed e5, then took f7. Now his knight forks my queen and rook.
    highlight piece_f7 piece_g5 piece_h8

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["Move the queen with a threat.","What stands behind the pawn on g2?"] themes ["trap","threat"] voice a test d18 teaches ["openings/traps","safety/threats"]
        right: Queen takes g2: she escapes the fork and hits the rook on h1. If the rook runs to f1, queen takes e4, check, and if the bishop blocks, knight to f3 is mate.
        wrong: Don't just save the queen: move her where she attacks.

A: Back to White. Légal's trap again, and this time Black took your knight, not your queen.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["Which black piece is loose?","The bishop that pinned your knight."] themes ["trap","hanging piece"] voice a test d18 teaches ["openings/traps","safety/hanging-pieces"]
        right: Queen takes h5: the pinning bishop was loose, and you're a pawn up.
        wrong: Your knight is gone. Which black piece has no guard now?

B: Refused bait, loose bishop. Same as before.
    highlight piece_h5

A: Next, the Petrov Defence. Black took your e-pawn, your queen came to e2, and his knight ran back to f6.
    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["Your queen and Black's king share the e-file.","Move the knight with check."] themes ["discovered attack","trap"] voice a test d18 teaches ["tactics/discovered-attack","openings/traps"]
        right: Knight to c6 uncovers check from your queen and hits Black's queen. The queen blocks on e7, the knight takes her, and the bishop takes back: whatever blocks, the queen falls.
        wrong: What is your knight hiding on the e-file?

A: The board turns again: you're Black. White's bishop has just taken yours on b4.
    animate position_shown 3 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["Checks first: your pawn on e3.","When it promotes, think: not a queen."] themes ["discovered attack","trap"] voice a test d18 teaches ["tactics/discovered-attack","openings/traps"]
        right: Pawn takes f2, check: if the king takes, your queen takes his. So the king goes to e2, and the pawn takes the knight on g1 and becomes a knight, with check.
        wrong: Look at every check, even a pawn's.

B: A knight, with check. I'd have made a queen.
    highlight piece_g1

A: A queen there is taken by the rook. The knight comes with check: that's the Lasker trap.
    highlight piece_h1 piece_e2

A: Last one: you're Black in the Two Knights, and White just took on d5. Which move dodges the Fried Liver?
    animate position_shown 4 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    quiz "Which move dodges the Fried Liver?" on_canvas true say_question false buttons_at.x 915 buttons_at.y 400 buttons_layout column voice a
        right: Knight to a5: it hits the bishop, and f7 stays safe. Knight takes d5 is the recapture White is hoping for.
        wrong: Which recapture lets White's knight take f7?
        * Nxd5
        + Na5

    erase position

    text score_big "Day 18 score" x 500 y 400 font_size 110

A: You scored {d18.score} of {d18.total}.
    results test d18

    pause 1

    erase score_big

A: So a trap is a gift with a question attached: before you take it, ask what it lets your opponent check or hit.
    text syn "Free?" x 500 y 400 font_size 150

    erase syn

A: Homework: one slow game today, fifteen plus ten or slower.
    text hw "15 + 10" x 500 y 450 font_size 72

A: Then find its first mistake.
    text hw2 "1st mistake?" x 500 y 330 font_size 56

